  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "hernia_mesh_section_1",
      "section_title": "Hernia mesh",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Overview",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/hernia_mesh_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/hernia_mesh_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Hernia mesh is a medical implant used to reinforce weakened tissue during hernia repair. It provides a scaffold that supports tissue healing and helps distribute forces across the repaired abdominal wall."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Unlike most instruments in this series, hernia mesh is an implantable surgical device, not a hand-held instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is commonly used in the repair of inguinal, femoral, umbilical, incisional, and other abdominal wall hernias. The choice of mesh depends on the hernia type, operative approach, anatomical location, contamination risk, and patient factors."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE identification statement:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a surgical hernia mesh, an implantable prosthetic material used to reinforce the abdominal wall during hernia repair. It provides mechanical support to the weakened tissue and promotes incorporation into the surrounding tissues, depending on the mesh material and design.”"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "Labelling and identification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/hernia_mesh_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/hernia_mesh_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/hernia_mesh_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A mesh specimen or product package may have the following identifiable features."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Label",
                    "Component or feature",
                    "Significance"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "A",
                      "Mesh body",
                      "Provides structural reinforcement"
                    ],
                    [
                      "B",
                      "Filaments or fibres",
                      "Form the supporting framework"
                    ],
                    [
                      "C",
                      "Pores or openings",
                      "Influence tissue integration and material flexibility"
                    ],
                    [
                      "D",
                      "Mesh edges",
                      "May be trimmed to fit the repair"
                    ],
                    [
                      "E",
                      "Coated surface, if present",
                      "May be designed to reduce adhesion to selected adjacent tissues"
                    ],
                    [
                      "F",
                      "Orientation markers, if present",
                      "Help identify the intended positioning or surface"
                    ],
                    [
                      "G",
                      "Package label",
                      "Identifies material, size, product details, and sterility information"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: Not all meshes are woven, have visible filaments, or possess a coating. Some are knitted, composite, partially absorbable, or made from biologic materials. Always identify the actual product."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "Properties and construction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Hernia meshes vary considerably in their composition and physical properties."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Material: May be synthetic, biologic, or biosynthetic."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Structure: May be knitted, woven, porous, or constructed as a composite."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pore size: Influences tissue ingrowth, flexibility, and the local tissue response."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Weight: Synthetic meshes may be categorized broadly as lightweight or heavyweight."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Flexibility: Must suit the anatomical site and the required repair."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Absorbability: Some products are permanent, while others have absorbable components."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Surface coating: Selected meshes have barriers designed to limit adhesion formation when placed near viscera."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sterility: Implantable mesh must be supplied and handled according to its sterile packaging and manufacturer's instructions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No single mesh design is best for every hernia repair."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_5",
              "group_title": "A. According to material",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/hernia_mesh_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "1. Synthetic non-absorbable mesh"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Examples include polypropylene and selected polyester meshes. These provide long-term reinforcement and are commonly used in abdominal wall hernia repair."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/hernia_mesh_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "2. Partially absorbable or composite mesh"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Combines materials with different properties. Some components remain while others are absorbed over time."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/hernia_mesh_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "3. Biologic mesh"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Made from processed biological tissue. It may be considered in selected complex repairs, although cost, recurrence risk, and clinical context must be considered."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/hernia_mesh_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "4. Biosynthetic or absorbable mesh"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Designed to degrade partly or completely over time. It is selected for particular clinical situations rather than as a universal replacement for permanent mesh."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_6",
              "group_title": "B. According to structure and design",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Macroporous mesh: Has relatively large pores that permit tissue ingrowth."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Lightweight mesh: Contains less material per unit area than a comparable heavyweight design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Heavyweight mesh: Generally contains more material and may have different stiffness and tissue-response characteristics."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Composite mesh: Combines materials or surfaces with different functions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Barrier-coated mesh: Designed to reduce adhesions when the product is intended for placement near abdominal viscera."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_7",
              "group_title": "C. According to operative approach",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Open hernia repair mesh."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Laparoscopic hernia repair mesh."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Robotic hernia repair mesh."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The surgical approach and the material are separate classification systems; a mesh is not defined solely by whether it is used in open or minimally invasive surgery."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Hernia mesh works by mechanical reinforcement and tissue integration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The hernia defect is identified and the repair is planned."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The mesh is selected and prepared according to the procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is positioned to reinforce the weakened area, with appropriate overlap as required by the technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It may be secured using sutures, tacks, glue, or another approved method, or placed using a technique that does not require direct fixation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Tissue healing and, for suitable meshes, tissue ingrowth provide longer-term support."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The mesh distributes mechanical stress over a larger area than the defect alone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key principle: Mesh reinforces the repair; it does not simply plug every hernia defect. The position, overlap, fixation, and choice of material are critical to the result."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "Uses and indications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Hernia mesh may be used for:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reinforcement of inguinal hernia repairs."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Femoral hernia repair."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Umbilical hernia repair."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Incisional hernia repair."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Selected epigastric and other ventral hernia repairs."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Complex abdominal wall reconstruction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Repair of selected recurrent hernias."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_10",
              "group_title": "Surgeries in which it is used",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Procedure",
                    "Example application"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Open inguinal hernia repair",
                      "Lichtenstein tension-free mesh repair"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Laparoscopic inguinal hernia repair",
                      "TEP or TAPP repair"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Umbilical hernia repair",
                      "Reinforcement of selected umbilical defects"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Incisional hernia repair",
                      "Abdominal wall reinforcement"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Ventral hernia repair",
                      "Selected open, laparoscopic, or robotic repairs"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Complex abdominal wall reconstruction",
                      "Selected repairs requiring prosthetic reinforcement"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clinical distinction: Mesh choice and placement must be adapted to the risk of infection, the presence of bowel contamination, the operative plane, and the individual patient's circumstances. Not every hernia requires mesh."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reduced tension: Mesh reinforcement can reduce the need to pull tissues together under excessive tension."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Lower recurrence risk: For many common adult hernias, appropriate mesh repair reduces recurrence compared with suture repair alone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mechanical support: Distributes forces across the repaired abdominal wall."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Versatility: Available in multiple shapes, sizes, and materials."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Minimally invasive options: Some products are suitable for laparoscopic or robotic procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Adaptability: Selected meshes can be tailored to fit the planned repair, according to the manufacturer's instructions."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Adds implant cost."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May cause chronic postoperative pain."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can become infected."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May contract or deform."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Some products may adhere to bowel or other viscera if used in an inappropriate position."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May require removal in selected severe complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Not all mesh types are suitable for contaminated operative fields."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Incorrect placement or inadequate overlap may contribute to recurrence."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "Complications and risks",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Explanation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Surgical-site or mesh infection",
                      "Infection may involve the surrounding tissues or implanted material"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Chronic postoperative pain",
                      "May result from nerve injury, fixation, fibrosis, or other causes"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Hernia recurrence",
                      "May occur because of inadequate coverage, fixation failure, tissue factors, or other technical and biological factors"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Mesh contraction or shrinkage",
                      "Changes in mesh dimensions may affect reinforcement or cause discomfort"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Adhesion formation",
                      "Mesh placed near viscera may adhere to bowel or other organs"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bowel erosion or fistula",
                      "Rare but serious complications can occur with certain placements or infections"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Seroma",
                      "Fluid may accumulate in the operative space"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Mesh migration or displacement",
                      "The mesh may move from its intended position"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Foreign-body reaction",
                      "The host tissue may react to the implanted material"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Mesh exposure or erosion",
                      "May occur in selected anatomical sites or following wound complications"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_14",
              "group_title": "Prevention",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Select an appropriate mesh for the clinical situation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Maintain strict aseptic technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use the correct operative plane and adequate coverage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Follow the product's instructions for trimming, positioning, and fixation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid inappropriate placement of uncoated mesh against viscera."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess infection risk and the degree of contamination before implanting mesh."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ensure appropriate postoperative follow-up."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Contraindications depend on the particular product and clinical context. Important considerations include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Known hypersensitivity to a material or component of the selected product."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Active infection at the intended implantation site."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Contaminated or dirty operative fields, where implant choice requires careful individualized assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Situations where the mesh cannot be placed or secured appropriately."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Product-specific restrictions on anatomical location or operative approach."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE point: Infection or contamination does not mean every mesh is universally prohibited in every circumstance. The decision depends on the patient, procedure, mesh type, and applicable surgical guidance."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "Handling, storage and sterilization",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Hernia mesh is an implant, so handling differs from that of reusable surgical instruments."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Check the product name, type, size, and expiry date."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect the sterile packaging for damage or compromise."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm the product is suitable for the intended anatomical site and procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Open the packaging using aseptic technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Handle the mesh with appropriate sterile instruments and gloves."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Trim or orient it only as permitted by the manufacturer's instructions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not resterilize or reuse a single-use mesh unless explicitly authorized under the product's validated instructions and applicable regulations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Document the implanted product according to local clinical practice."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Most packaged implantable meshes are supplied sterile. Do not assume that a mesh can be sterilized using the same method as a reusable stainless-steel instrument."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "hernia_mesh_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "hernia_mesh_group_17",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is hernia mesh?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An implantable prosthetic material used to reinforce a weakened abdominal wall during hernia repair."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the main principle of operation?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mechanical reinforcement of the hernia repair, with tissue integration for suitable materials."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Name four types of hernia mesh by material."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Synthetic non-absorbable, partially absorbable, biologic, and biosynthetic or absorbable mesh."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is a Lichtenstein repair?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An open tension-free inguinal hernia repair using mesh reinforcement of the posterior wall of the inguinal canal."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What do TEP and TAPP stand for?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "TEP means totally extraperitoneal repair; TAPP means transabdominal preperitoneal repair. Both are minimally invasive approaches to inguinal hernia repair."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Why is mesh used in many adult hernia repairs?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It reinforces the abdominal wall and generally reduces recurrence risk for many common hernias compared with suture repair alone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Mention five complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Infection, chronic pain, recurrence, seroma, and adhesion formation. Other complications include contraction, displacement, and, rarely, erosion or fistula."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. Why does mesh pore size matter?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pore size influences tissue ingrowth, flexibility, and the local tissue response."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. Why may a barrier-coated mesh be used?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Certain barrier-coated meshes are designed to reduce adhesion formation when placed adjacent to abdominal viscera, subject to the product's approved indications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q10. Is mesh always necessary for hernia repair?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. The decision depends on the hernia type, size, patient factors, contamination risk, and the chosen repair technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q11. Is hernia mesh a surgical instrument?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. It is an implantable surgical device."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q12. What must be checked before opening the package?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Product identity, size, expiry date, sterility information, and package integrity."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
